      "explanation": "False. Normal Group Policy processing follows Local, Site, Domain, then organizational units, from parent to child. A later applicable conflicting setting generally wins, so an OU policy normally overrides a domain setting and a domain setting normally overrides a local one. This enables centralized user and computer configuration. It is not simply whichever policy was edited most recently. Enforced links, blocked inheritance, security/WMI filtering, loopback, and some account-policy scopes require additional analysis; the ordinary precedence answer assumes those exceptions do not alter the case."
