      "explanation": "1. RAM is non-volatile. → No\n2. A low latency HDD is faster than an SSD. → No\n3. Physical storage includes SSD, HDD, and flash drives. → Yes\n\nOrdinary system RAM is volatile working memory used for active code and data; it is not long-term file storage. Restarting reinitializes the running environment, so the old runtime contents are not retained as the live session, although a restart is not a certified forensic overwrite of every RAM cell. HDDs, SSDs, and flash drives are physical non-volatile storage. SSDs generally have much lower random-access latency than mechanical HDDs, while RAM is faster still for typical access. CPU instructions execute in the processor, and the GPU handles graphics rather than replacing RAM or disk storage.",
