      "type": "dropdown",
      "question": "For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true and No if it\nis false. Assume normal Local–Site–Domain–OU processing, the same configuration scope, and no enforced links, blocked inheritance, loopback, or filtering exceptions.",
      "explanation": "1. For Group Policy, OU settings override domain settings. → Yes\n2. Group Policy does not override a local system policy. → No\n3. Group Policy provides central management of user and computer settings. → Yes\n\nNormal Group Policy processing follows Local, Site, Domain, then organizational units, from parent to child. A later applicable conflicting setting generally wins, so an OU policy normally overrides a domain setting and a domain setting normally overrides a local one. This enables centralized user and computer configuration. It is not simply whichever policy was edited most recently. Enforced links, blocked inheritance, security/WMI filtering, loopback, and some account-policy scopes require additional analysis; the ordinary precedence answer assumes those exceptions do not alter the case.",
