      "question": "Which two characteristics differentiate a Custom Installation of Windows from an Upgrade Installation? (Choose two)",
      "explanation": "An in-place upgrade attempts to preserve compatible applications, personal files, and settings while replacing Windows. A Custom installation installs a fresh copy on a selected partition and is appropriate for a clean deployment or an architecture change such as x86 to x64. Custom does not by itself guarantee secure erasure: formatting or deleting partitions is a separate, destructive choice, and existing files may otherwise be moved to Windows.old. Back up first. A Microsoft account is not the defining distinction, and domain joining can occur after installation rather than during it.",
