      "explanation": "1. Blank [1] → Sleep\n2. Blank [2] → Hibernate\n\nSleep keeps the active session in powered RAM and normally resumes quickly, making it suitable for a short absence. Hibernate writes the session to non-volatile storage and powers down, so the session can survive removal of power, usually with a slower resume. Shut down closes applications rather than preserving the working user session. Balanced is a power plan, not a saved-session state. Supported sleep states vary by hardware, and hybrid sleep or Modern Standby can alter behavior; ordinary sleep alone is not a promise of survival after all power is lost.",
