      "type": "single",
      "question": "An authorized user successfully copies decrypted files from NTFS to FAT32. Which NTFS-only security attributes cannot be retained on the destination?",
      "explanation": "FAT32 does not support NTFS discretionary access-control lists or EFS file encryption. When an authorized user successfully copies an EFS-encrypted file to FAT32 and permits decryption, the destination is plaintext without the original NTFS ACL. The source on NTFS is not automatically decrypted or stripped merely because a separate copy was made. If the user lacks a usable EFS private key or refuses a required decryption operation, the copy may fail; successful copying is explicitly assumed here. BitLocker volume encryption is a separate mechanism, not EFS retention on FAT32.",
