      "question": "A data manager is comparing the suitability of different file systems for various\nstorage needs. For each statement about NTFS, exFAT, and FAT32, select Yes if the\nstatement is true and No if it is false.",
      "explanation": "1. The FAT32 file system is limited by its inability to store individual files larger than 4GB. → Yes\n2. The NTFS file system is typically utilized when superior security features like file-level permissions and encryption are required. → Yes\n3. ExFAT (Extended File Allocation Table) is recommended for modern USB flash drives due to its universal compatibility and support for large file sizes. → No\n\nThe first two statements are Yes: FAT32 cannot store an individual file of 4 GiB or larger, and NTFS supports ACL-based file security and EFS on supported editions. The third is No because it claims universal exFAT compatibility. exFAT is useful for large files shared between many modern Windows/macOS systems, but older cameras, consoles, and embedded devices may not support it. A filesystem can support large files without being readable everywhere. FAT32 compatibility and NTFS security must be compared against the actual target devices, not a blanket “best” filesystem label.",
