      "question": "A technician is preparing to install Windows on a new laptop. For each statement about\nWindows installation scenarios, select Yes if the statement is true and No if it is\nfalse.",
      "explanation": "1. An Upgrade installation retains existing user files, application settings, and installed applications. → Yes\n2. A Custom installation is primarily used to install the operating system into a specific, selected partition. → Yes\n3. A domain-joined installation requires the computer to be connected to the Active Directory network during the installation process. → No\n\nAn in-place upgrade attempts to preserve compatible applications, personal files, and settings while replacing Windows. A Custom installation installs a fresh copy on a selected partition and is appropriate for a clean deployment or an architecture change such as x86 to x64. Custom does not by itself guarantee secure erasure: formatting or deleting partitions is a separate, destructive choice, and existing files may otherwise be moved to Windows.old. Back up first. A Microsoft account is not the defining distinction, and domain joining can occur after installation rather than during it.",
      "dragItems": [
        {
          "id": "yes",
          "text": "Yes"
        },
        {
          "id": "no",
          "text": "No"
        }
      ],
