      "question": "A technician is asked to identify different USB connector shapes used for peripherals.\nMatch each connector type from the list on the left to its physical description on the\nright.\n\n : The standard rectangular, legacy connector found on most host computers.\n : The small, oval-shaped connector known for its reversibility and high speed.\n : The square-shaped connector typically used on large devices like printers and\nscanners.",
      "explanation": "1. The standard rectangular, legacy connector found on most host computers. → USB Type-A\n2. The small, oval-shaped connector known for its reversibility and high speed. → USB Type-C\n3. The square-shaped connector typically used on large devices like printers and scanners. → USB Type-B\n\nUSB Type-A is the familiar rectangular host-side connector. USB Type-C has a small rounded, reversible shape; it can support modern high-speed modes, but its shape alone does not guarantee a speed. Standard USB Type-B is squarer and commonly appears on printers and scanners. Micro-USB is the smaller, flatter legacy mobile-device connector. The mappings are physical identification tasks, not a claim that a printer can only use Type-B or every smartphone now uses Type-C. Compatible cables must match both endpoints and the required data/power capabilities.",
