      "question": "A support staff member is reviewing the access control differences between user account\ntypes. For each statement about user permissions, select Yes if the statement is true\nand No if it is false.",
      "explanation": "1. An Administrative Account can install new hardware drivers and modify system-wide settings without requiring a User Account Control (UAC) prompt. → No\n2. A Standard User Account requires administrator credentials to install any application outside of the Microsoft Store. → No\n3. A Domain User Account provides full administrative privileges by default, solely by virtue of being part of the domain. → No\n\nAll three absolute privilege claims are false. Administrators normally still receive UAC consent prompts for operations requiring elevation under default settings. Standard users can install some per-user desktop applications outside the Store without administrator credentials; protected machine-wide changes are the operations requiring elevation. A domain account does not automatically receive local or domain administrative privileges. Identity source, account privilege, application installation scope, and UAC policy are distinct concepts and must not be treated as interchangeable labels.",
