      "question": "A developer is analyzing the Windows Registry structure. For each statement, select Yes\nif the statement is true and No if it is false.",
      "explanation": "1. The Registry is organized into a hierarchical structure of keys and subkeys. → Yes\n2. The Registry only stores settings for applications that were installed from the Microsoft Store. → No\n3. Modifying the Registry manually is the preferred method for changing user-level desktop preferences. → No\n\nThe Windows Registry is a hierarchical configuration database of keys, subkeys, and values used by Windows, hardware configuration, user profiles, and many applications. Persistent Registry settings normally survive restart because their hives are stored on disk; volatile keys are an exception. The Registry is not a repository of all documents, music, browser cache, or full system backups, and it does not replace RAM, antivirus, or filesystem repair tools. Prefer supported Settings or application controls over manual Registry editing, and back up relevant settings before an authorized low-level change.",
