      "explanation": "Programs and Features in Control Panel and the Apps area in Settings provide supported entry points for repairing, modifying, or uninstalling applications, when the application exposes those operations. A reinstall may require running the original installer again after removal. Task Manager controls execution, not installation repair, and Windows Update history records servicing rather than repairing arbitrary apps. Start can expose an uninstall command, but the question’s paired repair-management interfaces are Control Panel and Apps. Available buttons vary by app type and installer.",
