      "question": "A core benefit of implementing Active Directory in a corporate environment is its\nability to facilitate __. (Choose one)",
      "explanation": "Active Directory provides centralized identity and group management, and Group Policy applies authorized user/computer security and configuration rules. A domain-administrator account can perform broad directory administration, while an ordinary domain account does not gain those privileges solely through domain membership. Centralized management does not mean all personal files must physically reside on the domain controller. Routing and disk defragmentation are separate functions. Group Policy is the rules-delivery mechanism, the administrative account is a privileged identity, and centralized management is the broader organizational benefit.",
