      "chapter": "securitate-dispozitiv",
      "type": "drag_drop",
      "question": "A technician is formatting external drives for different use cases. Match each file system to its specific characteristic or limitation.\n\n : A modern file system optimized for flash drives that supports large files and is compatible with both Windows and macOS.\n : A legacy file system that provides broad compatibility but cannot store individual files larger than 4GB.\n : A proprietary Windows file system that supports file-level security, encryption, and compression.\n : A file system structure used primarily for optical media like DVDs.",
      "explanation": "1. A modern file system optimized for flash drives that supports large files and is compatible with both Windows and macOS. → exFAT\n2. A legacy file system that provides broad compatibility but cannot store individual files larger than 4GB. → FAT32\n3. A proprietary Windows file system that supports file-level security, encryption, and compression. → NTFS\n4. A file system structure used primarily for optical media like DVDs. → UDF\n\nNTFS is the normal Windows operating-system filesystem and supplies ACLs, EFS support, compression, and metadata journaling. FAT32 has broad legacy-device compatibility but limits one file to 4 GiB minus one byte. exFAT removes that small-file limit and is natively readable/writable on modern Windows and macOS, making it suitable for a 50 GB video transfer. UDF is commonly used for optical media. ReFS has specialized Windows uses and is not the broadly compatible cross-platform removable-drive choice. Filesystem selection does not itself provide an independent backup.",
