      "question": "Assume traditional 512-byte logical sectors for MBR and standard 64-bit Windows UEFI boot. Evaluate each statement.",
      "explanation": "1. The Master Boot Record (MBR) partition style supports drives larger than 2TB natively. → No\n2. GUID Partition Table (GPT) is required to boot Windows from a drive on a system using UEFI firmware. → Yes\n3. MBR is limited to a maximum of four primary partitions per drive. → Yes\n\nWith traditional 512-byte logical sectors, MBR addresses roughly 2 TiB and provides four primary partition-table entries. One entry can hold an extended partition containing multiple logical drives, so after three primary partitions an extended partition can hold the three additional logical drives requested. GPT supports larger disks and typically up to 128 partitions under Windows, rather than four primary entries. Standard 64-bit Windows boot in UEFI mode requires GPT. Converting the partition style is separate from formatting NTFS/FAT32; back up data and use supported conversion procedures before changing an existing disk.",
