      "chapter": "securitate-dispozitiv",
      "type": "single",
      "question": "A video editor needs to format a 1TB external SSD to transfer 50GB video files between a Windows 11 PC and a macOS laptop. The drive must support files larger than 4GB and be natively readable and writable by both operating systems without third-party drivers. Which file system should be selected? (Choose one)",
      "explanation": "NTFS is the normal Windows operating-system filesystem and supplies ACLs, EFS support, compression, and metadata journaling. FAT32 has broad legacy-device compatibility but limits one file to 4 GiB minus one byte. exFAT removes that small-file limit and is natively readable/writable on modern Windows and macOS, making it suitable for a 50 GB video transfer. UDF is commonly used for optical media. ReFS has specialized Windows uses and is not the broadly compatible cross-platform removable-drive choice. Filesystem selection does not itself provide an independent backup.",
      "options": [
        "exFAT",
        "NTFS",
        "FAT32",
        "ReFS"
      ],
      "correct": 0
