      "question": "What is a primary technical reason for an administrator to partition a single physical hard drive into two separate logical volumes (e.g., C: and D:)? (Choose one)",
      "explanation": "Separating the operating system and user data into different partitions can simplify a carefully scoped OS reinstall because the data partition can be left untouched. It does not protect against failure of the single physical disk, malware, or accidentally deleting the wrong partition; retain an independent backup. Partitioning does not inherently accelerate the disk mechanism, add RAM, or compress all data. The benefit is organization and selective maintenance, not a physical capacity or performance upgrade.",
