      "question": "A user creates a file on their local C: drive (NTFS) and then **moves** that file to a folder on a secondary D: drive (also NTFS). How does the file handle the permissions in the new location? (Choose one)",
      "explanation": "For the ordinary File Explorer operation assumed here, moving within the same NTFS volume retains the existing object and its permissions. Copying creates a new destination object, which normally inherits the destination folder’s permissions. A move to a different volume is effectively a copy followed by removal of the source, so the destination normally inherits permissions too. Permissions do not disappear or automatically become Everyone access. Tools that explicitly copy security descriptors, differing inheritance behavior, and non-NTFS destinations require separate analysis; the question excludes special ACL-preservation tools.",
