      "type": "drag_drop",
      "question": "Match the storage terminology on the left with its correct definition on the right.\n\n : A logical division of a hard drive that is treated as a separate unit by the operating system.\n : The process of preparing a partition with a file system (like NTFS) so it can store data.\n : A section of the hard drive allocated to support the virtual memory (RAM) of the system when physical RAM is full.\n : The method used to organize and track the location of files on a disk (e.g., NTFS, FAT32).",
      "explanation": "1. A logical division of a hard drive that is treated as a separate unit by the operating system. → Partition\n2. The process of preparing a partition with a file system (like NTFS) so it can store data. → Formatting\n3. A section of the hard drive allocated to support the virtual memory (RAM) of the system when physical RAM is full. → Pagefile / Swap\n4. The method used to organize and track the location of files on a disk (e.g., NTFS, FAT32). → File System\n\nA partition is a logical division of a disk, while formatting prepares a volume with a filesystem. The filesystem records how files and directories are organized and located. A Windows pagefile is a storage-backed component of virtual memory used for committed pageable data and other purposes; it is a file, not necessarily a dedicated partition. It does not physically add RAM or make disk access as fast as memory. These concepts describe disk layout, data organization, and memory support respectively, so partitioning and formatting should not be treated as the same operation.",
