      "explanation": "1. EFS encryption can be applied to files stored on a FAT32 formatted USB drive. → No\n2. A user with a suitable EFS private key, or a configured data recovery agent, can decrypt an EFS file when other access requirements are satisfied. → Yes\n3. Losing every usable EFS private key, including recovery keys and backups, leaves the encrypted data recoverable by normal means. → No\n\nEFS encrypts selected files on supported NTFS volumes, not FAT32. Decryption requires a usable authorized private key, including a configured data recovery agent where applicable, as well as other required access. NTFS ownership or administrator status alone does not supply that key. If every usable private key and recovery copy is lost, normal recovery cannot simply decrypt the data. Back up EFS certificates/private keys securely before changing accounts or reinstalling Windows. EFS is distinct from BitLocker volume encryption and from ACL permissions.",
