      "chapter": "acces-date",
      "type": "drag_drop",
      "question": "Match the backup or recovery feature on the left with its primary function on the right.\n\n : Captures selected system volumes, including Windows, apps, and settings, for whole-system restoration.\n : Automatically saves copies of files in the Documents, Music, Pictures, Videos, and Desktop folders to an external drive.\n : Reverts the computer's system files and settings to an earlier point in time without affecting personal files.\n : Syncs user folders (Desktop, Documents, Pictures) to the cloud to protect against local hardware failure.",
      "explanation": "1. Captures selected system volumes, including Windows, apps, and settings, for whole-system restoration. → System Image Backup\n2. Automatically saves copies of files in the Documents, Music, Pictures, Videos, and Desktop folders to an external drive. → File History\n3. Reverts the computer's system files and settings to an earlier point in time without affecting personal files. → System Restore\n4. Syncs user folders (Desktop, Documents, Pictures) to the cloud to protect against local hardware failure. → OneDrive Folder Backup\n\nA system image captures the selected system volumes for full-state recovery, including installed Windows and applications; it is not necessarily a forensic bit-for-bit clone of every physical disk sector. File History keeps versions of covered personal files on another drive or network location. System Restore rolls back monitored system state without targeting personal documents. OneDrive Folder Backup synchronizes supported user folders to the cloud and can protect against local drive failure, but syncing is not the same as an offline immutable backup. The four mappings differ in scope and restoration purpose.",
