      "id": "dcm-484",
      "chapter": "acces-date",
      "type": "single",
      "question": "A technician needs to create a new partition on a user's computer to store backup files. The disk currently has only one large \"C:\" partition that takes up the entire drive space, but it has 500GB of free space available. Which action in Disk Management must be performed on the C: volume first to make space available for the new partition? (Choose one)",
      "explanation": "Disk Management can change ordinary data-volume drive letters and shrink a supported basic volume to create unallocated space. That unallocated space can then hold a new partition; free space inside C: alone is not already a separate partition. Extend grows a volume instead of freeing space, and formatting destroys its filesystem contents rather than carving out unused capacity. Unmovable files and filesystem constraints can limit shrinking below the amount of reported free space. Changing the Windows system/boot drive letter is not an ordinary supported maintenance action.",
      "options": [
        "Extend Volume",
        "Format Volume",
        "Shrink Volume",
        "Change Drive Letter and Paths"
      ],
      "correct": 2
