      "question": "A user needs to manually connect to a network share using the Run dialog box. The server name is \"FileSrv\" and the share name is \"HR\". Which of the following represents the correct Universal Naming Convention (UNC) path syntax to access this share? (Choose one)",
      "explanation": "A UNC share path uses two leading backslashes, the server name, another backslash, and the share name, for example \\\\FileSrv\\HR. Mapping a drive adds a local letter referring to that remote path rather than relocating the data. A trailing dollar sign in a share name hides it from ordinary browsing lists but does not replace authorization: someone who knows the name may still attempt access. Public Folder Sharing is a convenience feature, not the most granular enterprise-security design. Colon paths and forward-slash web-style paths are not the requested SMB UNC syntax.",
