      "question": "A technician is studying how Windows handles conflicting permissions. For each statement about NTFS permission logic, select Yes if the statement is true and No if it is false.",
      "explanation": "1. Permissions assigned to a user directly and permissions assigned to the groups they belong to are cumulative (added together). → Yes\n2. An explicit \"Deny\" permission takes precedence over an \"Allow\" permission, blocking access even if the user is in a group with Full Control. → Yes\n3. When copying a file from an NTFS drive to a FAT32 USB stick, the file retains its original Access Control List (ACL) permissions. → No\n\nWithin NTFS, applicable Allow rights from the user and groups normally accumulate: Read plus Write gives both, and Read plus Modify remains Modify. An applicable explicit Deny blocks the conflicting requested right in the assumed canonical ACL; it does not necessarily remove unrelated allowed rights. For SMB network access, intersect that NTFS result with the share result. Thus permissive share rights cannot defeat an NTFS deny, while a mere Read Allow in one group does not reduce a Modify Allow in another. FAT32 cannot preserve an NTFS ACL when a file is copied there.",
