      "type": "drag_drop",
      "question": "Match each non-technical security threat to its correct description.\n\n : Gathering information by looking over someone's shoulder as they work or enter a password.\n : Searching through trash or recycling bins to find sensitive documents or information.\n : Manipulating people into giving up confidential information (e.g., calling and pretending to be IT support).",
      "explanation": "1. Gathering information by looking over someone's shoulder as they work or enter a password. → Shoulder Surfing\n2. Searching through trash or recycling bins to find sensitive documents or information. → Dumpster Diving\n3. Manipulating people into giving up confidential information (e.g., calling and pretending to be IT support). → Social Engineering\n\nSocial engineering manipulates people rather than relying only on software exploits. Phishing commonly uses deceptive email, vishing uses voice calls, and smishing uses SMS. Spoofing falsifies an apparent sender or caller identity; clone phishing reuses a plausible existing message with malicious changes. Shoulder surfing observes someone’s screen or input, while dumpster diving searches discarded material. These distinctions explain the mappings by channel and mechanism: a fraudulent caller is not a trash search, and falsifying a sender is not the same as physically stealing a device.",
