      "explanation": "Use explicit Deny carefully because it can block a conflicting Allow received through another group and unexpectedly lock out legitimate users, including administrators working under the affected identity. It does not reduce network bandwidth or require an extra Windows component, and it is not limited to Public folders. Prefer a comprehensible least-privilege allow design when possible. Canonical ACL ordering, inheritance, and the particular requested right matter; a deny of Write should not be described as automatically removing all unrelated read or security-management capabilities.",
