      "chapter": "securitate-dispozitiv",
      "type": "single",
      "question": "How does a Trojan Horse typically compromise a system? (Choose one)",
      "explanation": "A classic virus attaches to host code and replicates when that code executes; user activity commonly initiates that execution. A worm is standalone and can propagate automatically, often exploiting vulnerable services. A Trojan gains trust by masquerading as legitimate software but does not have to self-replicate or always install a backdoor. A logic bomb waits for a specified time, event, or condition rather than necessarily running immediately on download. These categories describe propagation or concealment mechanisms, and real malware can combine several behaviors; not every harmful program fits only one label.",
