      "id": "dcm-635",
      "chapter": "securitate-dispozitiv",
      "type": "single",
      "question": "How does a DDoS attack differ from a DoS attack? (Choose one)",
      "explanation": "A denial-of-service attack targets availability by exhausting resources or otherwise preventing legitimate service. A distributed DoS uses multiple sources, often a botnet; a basic single-source DoS does not require that distributed infrastructure. Stealing credentials, encrypting files for payment, and physically damaging hardware describe different goals. Large volumetric attacks can saturate the upstream connection before a home software firewall can help, so mitigation often requires the ISP or an upstream scrubbing service. DDoS does not always require infected hosts, since reflection and other distributed sources are also possible.",
