      "type": "single",
      "question": "Why is an \"Errant USB drive\" attack so effective? (Choose one)",
      "explanation": "A planted or abandoned USB drive can exploit curiosity or helpfulness to cause someone to attach attacker-controlled hardware. Do not plug it into a work computer just to identify the owner, scan it, or format it; secure it and hand it to the authorized security team. Disabling AutoRun reduces one route but does not stop every USB attack, such as malicious keyboard emulation. Full-disk encryption protects stored data after theft when the device is appropriately locked/off, and a privacy screen reduces shoulder surfing. A screen-capture device can be physical hardware rather than only software.",
