      "chapter": "acces-date",
      "type": "drag_drop",
      "question": "Move each USB connector type from the list on the left to its correct description on the right.\n\n : Reversible oval connector commonly used on modern Windows 11 laptops and smartphones.\n : Standard rectangular connector found on most older legacy PCs and flash drives.\n : Square-like connector typically used to connect desktop printers and scanners to a PC.\n : Small, flat connector commonly used for older legacy mobile devices and accessories before Type-C.",
      "explanation": "1. Reversible oval connector commonly used on modern Windows 11 laptops and smartphones. → USB Type-C\n2. Standard rectangular connector found on most older legacy PCs and flash drives. → USB Type-A\n3. Square-like connector typically used to connect desktop printers and scanners to a PC. → USB Type-B\n4. Small, flat connector commonly used for older legacy mobile devices and accessories before Type-C. → Micro-USB\n\nUSB Type-A is the familiar rectangular host-side connector. USB Type-C has a small rounded, reversible shape; it can support modern high-speed modes, but its shape alone does not guarantee a speed. Standard USB Type-B is squarer and commonly appears on printers and scanners. Micro-USB is the smaller, flatter legacy mobile-device connector. The mappings are physical identification tasks, not a claim that a printer can only use Type-B or every smartphone now uses Type-C. Compatible cables must match both endpoints and the required data/power capabilities.",
