      "type": "dropdown",
      "question": "For each statement about Storage Spaces in Windows 11, select True or False.",
      "explanation": "1. Storage Spaces allows you to group multiple physical drives into a single logical pool. → Yes\n2. You can use Storage Spaces to protect your data from drive failures by mirroring files. → Yes\n3. Storage Spaces can only combine drives of the exact same size and connection type. → No\n\nStorage Spaces groups supported physical drives into a storage pool and creates virtual disks with selected resiliency such as mirroring or parity. Drives need not have exactly identical capacities, although mismatched sizes and hardware affect usable capacity and performance. Parity requires enough eligible drives and is not RAID 0, which has no redundancy. Dynamic disks are a different legacy storage mechanism, and the pagefile supports virtual memory rather than pooling disks. Resiliency helps with supported drive failures but is not a backup against deletion, ransomware, or disaster.",
