      "type": "dropdown",
      "question": "For each statement about connecting peripherals in Windows 11, select True or False.",
      "explanation": "1. Windows 11 can automatically detect and install drivers for many Plug and Play devices. → Yes\n2. Bluetooth peripherals such as headsets normally require a supported pairing workflow before use; Settings is one common way to pair them. → Yes\n3. A standard USB Type-A device can be plugged directly into a USB Type-C port without an adapter. → No\n\nPlug and Play can detect many supported peripherals and install an available built-in or distributed driver, but it does not guarantee every device works without a vendor package. Bluetooth peripherals such as headsets normally need pairing and an enabled radio; some use Swift Pair or other supported workflows rather than manually navigating Settings. Type-A and Type-C are physically different connector shapes and do not directly fit each other without an appropriate adapter or cable. Detection, pairing, and physical compatibility are separate prerequisites.",
