      "explanation": "1. Identifying if a remote server is online and responding. → ping\n2. Viewing the path a packet takes to reach its destination. → tracert\n3. Seeing the current IP address assigned to your PC. → ipconfig\n4. Requesting a new IP address from the DHCP server. → ipconfig /renew\n\nping tests whether a destination responds to ICMP echo, while tracert uses TTL-limited probes to reveal responding hops and their measured round-trip times. ipconfig reports local addressing, and ipconfig /renew requests a DHCP lease. nslookup queries DNS rather than tracing the path. A nonresponse can result from filtering or rate limiting, and a slow intermediate probe does not necessarily prove application traffic is delayed there. Use the tool matching the requested evidence and interpret it alongside connectivity and service tests.",
