      "id": "dcm-702",
      "chapter": "aplicatii-periferice",
      "type": "dropdown",
      "question": "Evaluate the following statements regarding the installation and removal of third-party software on Windows. Choose True or False for each.",
      "explanation": "1. During installation, third-party software is restricted from modifying the Windows Registry. → No\n2. Removing an application's desktop shortcut will completely uninstall the program from your system. → No\n3. Windows automatically separates the default installation folders for third-party programs depending on whether they are 32-bit or 64-bit. → Yes\n\nThird-party installers can write application configuration and registrations into the Windows Registry, subject to permissions and elevation. Deleting a desktop shortcut removes only a link to the program, not the program or its Registry entries. Use a supported uninstaller instead of deleting shortcuts or installation folders. On 64-bit Windows, conventional system-wide directories separate 32-bit programs in Program Files (x86) from native 64-bit programs in Program Files, although installers can choose exceptions. These statements concern installation conventions and persistent configuration, not merely whether an app is currently running.",
