      "explanation": "1. The primary function of a graphics card is to process visual data and output it to a display device. → Yes\n2. Common connection ports found on video cards include VGA, DVI, HDMI, and DisplayPort. → Yes\n3. An integrated GPU comes with its own dedicated VRAM separate from the system's main memory. → No\n4. A graphics card is capable of directly accessing computer peripherals to increase the CPU's overall processing performance. → No\n\nA GPU processes graphics for display output. Video cards can provide VGA, DVI, HDMI, or DisplayPort depending on age and model; not every card provides every connector. Integrated graphics usually shares system RAM rather than always having separate dedicated VRAM, while discrete cards commonly have their own video memory. Installing a graphics card does not guarantee a faster CPU clock or arbitrary direct control of all peripherals. Graphics acceleration can offload suitable workloads, but that is different from upgrading the processor’s intrinsic capabilities.",
