      "type": "single",
      "question": "You need to format a 4 GB flash drive on Windows 11 so that it can be read by older digital cameras, various gaming consoles, and macOS or Linux machines. What is the most universally compatible file system for this purpose?",
      "explanation": "FAT32 is the best listed choice for a small flash drive that must work with many older cameras, consoles, and non-Windows systems. Its individual-file limit of 4 GiB minus one byte is a trade-off, but the use case emphasizes broad legacy compatibility rather than huge files. exFAT is useful on modern compatible devices but is not supported by every older device. NTFS, ReFS, and ext4 have different native support and are not the widest legacy-device answer. Format only after backing up existing data and checking the target device’s requirements.",
