      "question": "Assess the following facts regarding the Active Directory domain administrator role. Choose True or False.",
      "explanation": "1. Domain Administrators automatically bypass every file permission and encryption barrier without any additional action or key. → No\n2. Domain administrators can create domain user accounts and assign authorized permissions. → Yes\n3. Domain administrator accounts can never be locked out under any account or policy circumstances. → No\n\nDomain Admins normally receive broad domain-management authority and, by default, local administrative membership on domain-joined member computers. They can create domain users and assign authorized rights. That does not automatically bypass every NTFS ACL, supply an EFS private key, or exempt all members from account lockout. Built-in account exceptions and policy specifics differ from blanket claims about the whole group. Use privileged accounts only for authorized administration; broad authority is not a reason to use them for routine browsing or office work.",
